<speak>
<p>Let u be odd and greater than one. Our all-ones polynomial J of P is the sum of P to the r, from r equal to zero through u squared minus one. Let I contain the distinct primes dividing u. Choose a primitive p-th root for each prime p. For a subset S, multiply its chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S. The empty subset gives the input one; set it aside for the next node. Our target is exact zero at every nonempty-subset input.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For a nonempty subset, put m equal to the product of its distinct primes. The root zeta sub S raised to m equals one, so its order divides m. Fix a prime p in S. Raising to m over p removes all the other root factors. The remaining factor is zeta sub p raised to m over p. This is not one, because p does not divide m over p. The order therefore contains every prime in the squarefree number m, and must equal m.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For example, take u equal to fifteen and S containing three and five. The fifth power of the product of zeta three and zeta five leaves zeta three to the fifth, which is nontrivial. The third power leaves zeta five cubed, also nontrivial. The product has order fifteen. In general, nonempty S gives m greater than one, and m divides u squared. Thus zeta sub S is not one, while its u-squared power is one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Now use a formal polynomial identity. Multiply J of P by P minus one. The shifted row P times J has powers one through u squared. Subtracting J cancels every matching power from P through P to the exponent u squared minus one. Only P raised to u squared, minus one, remains. Therefore the product of P minus one and J of P equals the difference between P raised to u squared and one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Substitute the root. Its u-squared power is one, so zeta sub S minus one, multiplied by J of zeta sub S, equals zero. These evaluated values lie in the characteristic-zero domain A inside the complex numbers. The first factor is nonzero. Cancel it in that domain to obtain J of zeta sub S equal to zero. This is exact, before any reduction modulo one plus i.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For u equal to three, let omega be the exponential of two pi i over three. The nine powers repeat one, omega, and omega squared three times. J of omega equals three times the quantity one plus omega plus omega squared, which is zero.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Draw the three unit vectors in each group head to tail. Each triple closes into an equilateral triangle, so its sum is zero. All three groups vanish exactly. The geometric-sum argument proves the same cancellation for every nonempty subset. The waiting input P equal to one is excluded from that conclusion. It behaves differently, and the next node evaluates it.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
